ABSTRACT
Rocket components manufactured using Additive Manufacturing are becoming increasingly popular in the space
industry due to their commercial and performance advantages. The development and maintenance of orbital
launch capabilities and allied technologies have historically been expensive, with few countries successfully
achieving these goals. However, recent advances in Additive Manufacturing and other technologies have made
space-based activities more accessible. This thesis reports both on the characterisation of materials, and the
development of components for rocket engine applications for the South African First Integrated Rocket Engine
(SAFFIRE) programme. This thesis aimed to gain insight and experience into the potential applications of
Additive Manufacturing, with a focus on practical solutions and quality control.
For the characterization of materials, the methodology used comprised of the manufacture of standardized cube
samples, overhang samples and wall-thickness specimens to be individually analysed using the non-destructive
testing method, microcomputed tomography. Porosity and surface roughness were of particular interest in this
investigation to determine the defects present in the Additively Manufactured materials evaluated. Applications
of these Additively Manufactured materials were then manufactured and investigated with respect to impeller and
volute pump components, and an injector flange. Using microcomputed tomography methods, these applications
were evaluated to quantify surface deviation and other Additive Manufacturing process-based defects.
Furthermore, the effects of Design for Additive Manufacturing techniques on these applications were investigated.
Both the quantitative and qualitative results for porosity and surface roughness exhibited acceptable quality for
all the materials under investigation, except for the 6061-T6 samples. For the 6061-T6 samples, porosity and
surface deviation results were of concern, and Additive Manufacturing process parameter modifications were
implemented which reduced these defect occurrences. For the impeller, volute and injector flange component
applications, various defects were quantified and mitigated primarily through the use of Design for Additive
Manufacturing techniques. The results in this thesis provide valuable insights into the specific quality of the
Additive Manufacturing processes and materials under investigation. In the context of the material
characterization, the results provide clarity on the quality of additively manufactured materials available in the
South African supply chain. These standardized sample testing methods can easily be applied to future Additive
Manufacturing material investigations, where specific material properties are not well known. The process
parameter modification and quality control techniques presented in this thesis can similarly be applied to future
Additive Manufacturing material investigations. For the component applications presented, the observed defects
provided an understanding of the formation of these defects, and the adopted mitigation strategies showed clear
improvements in quality, which may be applicable to future component applications.


