1. INTRODUCTION
1.1. Space Industry
The space industry and its constituents have historically been expensive endeavours with only a handful of
countries successfully developing and maintaining national programs for orbital launch capabilities and other
related space-based activities. However, recent advances in microelectronics, cost-effective manufacturing,
metallurgy, computational design and analyses have made space-based activities more accessible than ever before.
The small satellite market, in particular, has seen tremendous growth since 2012 and the market is expected to
grow considerably with an estimated 7,000 small satellites likely to be launched between the years 2018 and 2027
[1]. Small satellites typically have a mass range of 1-500 kg and therefore do not require large orbital launch
vehicles commonly used in the traditional space industry. This has allowed many nations to embark on the
development of national and/or commercial launch capabilities since smaller orbital launch vehicle programmes
are considerably cheaper to develop, test and maintain.
1.2. South African First Integrated Rocket Engine (SAFFIRE) Programme Overview
With no current orbital launch capabilities established in Africa, there is a keen interest within South Africa to
develop such a capability in order support socioeconomic advancement of the continent. The Aerospace Systems
Research Group (ASReG) located at the University of KwaZulu-Natal was formed in 2009 with the purpose of
designing, manufacturing and testing rocket propulsion systems. The South African First Integrated Rocket
Engine (SAFFIRE) project at ASReG aims to develop liquid rocket engines for a small satellite launch vehicle
configured to deliver payloads to orbit from South Africa. With ongoing development work on rocket engine
technology, as well as allied systems and infrastructure, the SAFFIRE programme is developing the local skills
and knowledge necessary to support the establishment of an indigenous orbital launch capability.
1.3. Additive Manufacturing
The vast majority of orbital launch vehicles employ liquid fuel rocket engines for propulsion. These engines have
traditionally been extremely complex and expensive to develop, manufacture and test. In this regard, Additive
Manufacturing (AM) techniques present a wide variety of benefits such as light-weighting, part consolidation,
cost reduction, design sophistication, and reduced lead times [2]. AM techniques are relatively new, with the first
application for a spacecraft being launched on the Juno mission to Jupiter in 2011 [3]. However, AM technology
has developed rapidly over the last decade and currently, the majority of orbital launch providers are either
developing or using AM for various launch vehicle components. A good example of this is Rocket Lab’s
Rutherford liquid fuel rocket engine, which has been using AM techniques to manufacture all of their primary
engine components such as combustion chambers, injectors, pumps, and main propellant valves since 2013 [4].


