1.4. Problem Statement and Research Objectives
Based on previous contributions to the SAFFIRE programme and current market developments, it is clear that
utilizing Additive Manufacturing (AM) in the production process for liquid rocket engine components has some
distinct advantages. Although some components have been designed and manufactured using AM techniques by
ASReG, the expertise required to execute the development of highly accurate and complex components was not
available. This lack of practical experience gave rise to this project. In collaboration with both the Stellenbosch
University CT Scanner Facility and the Collaborative Program for Additive Manufacturing (CPAM), further
expertise and funding was utilized for the purposes of Non-Destructive Testing (NDT) and Design for Additive
Manufacturing (DfAM) techniques. The Stellenbosch University collaborator (co-supervisor) has ongoing
research efforts funded by the CPAM program, investigating the structural integrity of metal AM parts and
evaluating their performance based on defects identified non-destructively, and ultimately enhancing the quality
of such AM parts for critical applications. This student project forms an important aspect of this program, with
great synergy between the expertise of the groups involved to make progress in this area of application. The
primary aim of this study is to gain insight and experience in the AM production process to produce liquid rocket
engine components according to specified requirements.
While the AM industry and its applications are growing extensively worldwide, the adoption of this technology
is still in its infancy. Manufacturing aerospace components requires careful examination and inspection during
the manufacturing process. This due diligence is lacking in the South African AM industry and requires a strict
framework for manufacturing standards similar to those of the International Organization of Standardization
(ISO). Aerospace components in particular have much stricter standards generally when compared to those of
other industries, and the international standards related to AM components in the space industry are mostly new
or still in development.
AM enables the creation of extremely complex geometries which are not possible using traditional manufacturing
methods. As with all manufacturing methods, the final manufactured product may deviate from the design in
several ways, or may feature unexpected defects or flaws. In AM, this occurs mostly in the form of geometric
deviation (e.g. due to thermal warpage), surface roughness irregularity, internal porosity and cracks forming
during the build process. These defects require advanced evaluation techniques such as Non-Destructive Testing
(NDT) to assess the internal defects (porosity and cracks) and external defects (geometric deviation and surface
roughness irregularity).
The quality of components manufactured using AM techniques also differs widely depending on the material
used. Liquid rocket engine components are often made from high performance alloys which mostly can be
manufactured using AM processes. However, due to the low margins for error and strict tolerances required on
such components, extensive testing is required to ensure the quality of the AM process for each material under
consideration.
AM presents some unique advantages in the design and manufacturing process in the form of rapid prototyping
and cost-effective production at small scale production volumes. This synergizes with the space industry as most
components are high-value and low production volume. All engineering components are somewhat limited by the
production method used to fabricate them, and the associated capital investment required. Using AM to iteratively


