3. AM PROCESS AND MATERIALS CHARACTERIZATION FOR AEROSPACE
APPLICATIONS
3.1. Introduction
Processing of Additive Manufacturing (AM) materials by Laser Powder Bed Fusion (L-PBF) and other AM
techniques has advanced significantly in recent years, particularly in terms of process optimization and material
property characterization. A wide range of aerospace, biomedical, and industrial fields are interested in the as
manufactured material properties characteristic of AM due to the performance and cost-effectiveness of the
process. Despite this, AM components often contain defects, which affect mechanical properties [432]. These
defects occur due to a variety of causes such as imperfect processing parameters, unoptimized geometry, incorrect
post-processing and contamination.
For the aerospace and space industries, AM has been shown to provide benefits such as cost savings and high
performance when compared to traditional manufacturing techniques (Chapter 2). In addition to typically being
high-cost, lightweight and high-stress, spacecraft components are often exposed to multi-physics loading
conditions that create local stress states that are hard to predict. As a result of the uncertainties associated with
AM materials, design processes for components are often highly test dependent. Before production of flight
weight, full-scale components, small-scale components are often designed, manufactured, and tested.
As part of the Aerospace Systems Research Group (ASReG), the South African First Integrated Rocket Engine
(SAFFIRE) programme aims to produce functional rocket engines for the purpose of powering an orbital class
rocket for delivering small satellites to orbit. Due to the high-stress nature of spacecraft components, the fidelity
of mechanical properties of their materials of construction is very important. Incorporating new technologies such
as AM into a rocket engine poses many challenges with respect to confidence in material properties. A particularly
effective way to increase the confidence in manufactured components through AM is to use non-destructive
testing (NDT) techniques such as micro computed tomography (Micro-CT). It is common to use MicroCT in the
AM industry, but the process is often time-consuming and costly, thus decreasing its potential impact and benefits.
Standardized test procedures can reduce analysis time and costs associated with Micro-CT and improves
confidence regarding results [433].
As opposed to traditional manufacturing processes, AM parts and material processes have not yet had many years
of incremental refinement by third parties. In addition, a range of factors affect the end quality. In order to ensure
consistency and manage risks associated with the process, a disciplined methodology must be established to
control these variables. While standards related to the qualification of AM materials and processes do exist, most
were developed recently or are still in development. Table 2-2 lists several examples of these.
This chapter describes the process by which the AM materials evaluated in this study were tested and classified
through NDT techniques, in order to improve the confidence in the mechanical properties of the materials
considered for use in the SAFFIRE programme.


48
3.2. Additive Manufacturing Materials
AM materials, and particularly, metal AM materials, present distinct advantages for aerospace applications. While
AM is still a relatively recent technology, many processing technologies and materials have been developed with
high technology readiness levels (TRL) and extensive research is being performed to increase the range of
materials and processes available to the AM industry. Due to the particular advantages that AM introduces to
aerospace applications, the majority of the materials with extensive development in the AM industry are useful
aerospace engineering alloys. Section 2.2.2 provides an in-depth review of AM materials available and their use
cases for aerospace applications.
A critical aspect of spacecraft component success is the selection of materials, which is often dependent on the
design methodology and manufacturing process used. The materials used for spacecraft are often exposed to
severe conditions such as extreme temperatures and heavy loads. Typically, temperatures vary from cryogenic (
253 °C) to extremely high (over 1,000 °C). The specific environments that spaceflight components are subjected
to vary widely and depend on the nature of the component in question, which may fall into the following
categories: rotating turbomachinery, injectors, valves, pressurized tanks, combustion chambers, feedlines, and
various structural components. In spacecraft component environments, propellants or combustion products are
often present and under high pressure which poses the risk of corrosion, embrittlement, or oxidation. Additionally,
a reliable determination of material properties is dependent on both the maturity of the material and the maturity
of the processing associated with the material. With respect to the SAFFIRE programme, there exist potential AM
applications for all of the above-mentioned component categories.
The South African AM supply chain, while still in its infancy, enables the manufacture a variety of useful
aerospace materials with high levels of maturity. This is, however, limited to common Laser Powder Bed Fusion
(L-PBF) platforms such as the EOS M280 (see details in Appendix C: EOS M280 Additive Manufacturing
Machine Specifications). Other AM processes such as Directed Energy Deposition (DED) have some potential
applications in the SAFFIRE programme, however, the supply chain in South Africa for this method is
underdeveloped. In the context of the SAFFIRE programme, L-PBF introduces the most relevant advantages such
as feature resolution, surface finish and mechanical properties, and is therefore the primary AM process under
investigation in this chapter. The testing methods proposed here can however be applied to AM processes such as
DED.
The materials investigated as part of the work presented in this chapter were also selected on the basis of their
potential value to the SAFFIRE programme. These four materials are Ti-6Al-4V, 316L Stainless Steel, MS1
Maraging Steel and Aluminium 6061-T6 RAM2. Each of these materials is readily available via the South African
AM supply chain for the L-PBF process. The specific application areas, advantages and maturities are listed
below.
Titanium Ti-6Al-4V is a widely adopted aerospace alloy and one of the most researched AM materials. It has high
specific strength, excellent corrosion resistance and high-temperature stability [434]. These characteristics provide
significant lightweighting opportunities for aerospace applications, however, Ti-6Al-4V is highly reactive in
oxygen-enriched environments and therefore its application areas are limited to those not exposed to oxygen-rich


49
fluids. This material has the greatest maturity both in the South African AM supply chain and globally
[38,435,436].
316L Stainless Steel is a common alloy used in most industries due to its relatively low cost, durability, corrosion
resistance, high-temperature stability, cryogenic temperature stability and oxidation resistance [62]. 316L is
compatible with liquid oxygen (LOX) and is therefore of interest to application environments where LOX is
present. This material exhibits low relative specific strength at room temperature when compared to other AM
materials and should therefore be used sparingly where lightweighting is of importance. 316L is a commonly
manufactured alloy on L-PBF systems and has high maturity.
MS1 Maraging Steel is an AM-specific alloy similar to tool steels such as European 1.2709 and German
X3NiCoMoTi 18-9-5, which are used in many industries. It has high specific strength, hardness, corrosion
resistance, stiffness and durability [60,61]. In its heat-treated state, this material exhibits excellent strength
characteristics, however, it is highly brittle in this state. The maturity of this material is high but limited to the
EOS M280 platform.
Aluminium 6061-T6 RAM2 (Appendix G: Elementum3D 6061-T6 RAM2 Powder/Material Specifications) is a
general-purpose AM alloy that features a combination of high specific strength, good thermal conductivity, high
ductility, low-cost, and low-temperature stability. The alloy was recently developed by Elementum3D and is one
of the first 6061 alloys successfully developed for AM processes. However, due to the novelty of the material, its
mechanical properties and AM process quality are not well known and are thus a particular focus in this chapter.
