AM suppliers. The following heat-treatment processes were employed for each material evaluated:
• Ti-6Al-4V – Stress relieving at 650 °C for 3 hours. Annealing at 941 °C for 2 hours
• MS1 - Solution treatment at 940 °C (1724 °F) for 2 hours. Age-hardening at 490 °C (914 °F) for 6 hours
• 316L - Stress relieving at 900 °C for 2 hours, followed by water quenching. Solution annealing at
1 150 °C hold for 1.5 hours, followed by water quenching
• 6061-T6 – Solution treatment at 500 0C for 9-10 hours, followed by water quenching. Precipitation
hardening at 160 °C (320 °F) for 18 hours, followed by air cooling. Further precipitation hardening at
177 °C (350 °F) for 8 hours, followed by air cooling.
3.3. Common AM Defects
The formation of defects in AM-built components used in critical structural applications is of major concern.
Therefore, knowledge of the mechanisms of defect formation in L-PBF and other AM processes is crucial to
determining the appropriate use cases specific to the selected alloy system and processing technique. It has been
demonstrated that powder-based AM can be applied in many fields such as the aerospace and medical industries
[437]. A suitable AM part should have at least the same, or preferably better, quality as a conventionally
manufactured part. Detailed knowledge of the defect types, their mechanisms of occurrence, and associated
detection methodologies is essential to the evaluation and control of mechanical properties. In fusion-based
processes, defects can include keyhole collapse, gas porosity, solidification cracking, solid-state cracking, and


50
surface-connected porosity [437,438]. It is possible to eliminate such defects using defect-mitigation techniques
such as post-process machining, surface treatment, and post-processing HIP [437,439]. The two primary defect
types that are considered in this chapter are porosity and surface roughness.
3.3.1. Porosity
Voids are typically defined as regions containing “nothing” either inside, or outside of an object under inspection.
In manufacturing, pores, similarly to voids, can be defined as regions where the intended material/s is not present.
For AM processes, pores are often spherical in shape, and contain process gasses such as Argon. These pores can
be formed within a single layer, between adjacent layers and/or on the external surface of the build part. In addition
to pores, other microstructural inhomogeneities can occur in AM-built parts, such as impurities (inclusions,
contaminations, and metal oxides), un-melted or partially melted particles, and abnormal grain growth. In this
chapter, the effects of pores and how pores can be identified through MicroCT imaging are specifically examined.
Pores can be formed for several reasons which are discussed in greater detail in [440–443].
The presence of manufacturing faults, such as porosity, can adversely affect the mechanical properties of final
products, regardless of whether they are manufactured by additive manufacturing or not. In terms of mechanical
properties, a range of specific properties are of concern, including modulus of elasticity, yield strength, ultimate
tensile strength, ductility, toughness, and hardness, under static/quasi-static conditions. It is typically desirable to
have both a high number of crack initiation cycles and high crack growth resistance under dynamic loading, both
of which are affected by porosity characteristics. There are typically standards for wrought or cast materials for
each material type that can be used as a guide to ascertain how L-PBF materials should be manufactured. The
earliest L-PBF optimization studies reported, at best, a relative density of 87% (or 13% porosity) [444], with future
improvements resulting in significantly higher relative densities, typically reaching values greater than 99% (or
less than 1% porosity) [445]. It is, however, important to know how the desired mechanical properties are affected
by porosity, pore size, and distribution, and the extent to which these characteristics threaten critical applications,
such as those in the medical or aerospace fields.
The defects present on AM-built materials often resembles that of cast metals, and consideration of cast metals
provides useful insight into the effects of defects on mechanical properties. Casting porosity is well known to
result in poor mechanical properties such as reduced tensile strength and ductility, according to studies such as
those highlighted in [446]. According to static tensile tests monitored by X-ray tomography, failure almost always
occurs at the largest pore, and pore size is inversely related to effective yield strength and ductility [447]. For AM
process, and with properly selected process parameters and scanning strategies, the significance of porosity
associated with L-PBF can be greatly reduced, to the extent that the mechanical properties of the material aren’t
significantly affected. According to [448], porosity values of up to 1% tended to have a small effect on tensile
strength and elongation to failure. Furthermore, even with pores up to 4 mm in diameter, yield strength was still
comparable to the wrought standards [447].
Porosity is of particular interest in this study for the purposes of determining the specific defects present in the
AM materials evaluated here, and how they influence mechanical properties. These defects have mitigation


51
strategies such as optimal heat treatment or HIP techniques, and characterising porosity for specific component
and material combinations informs critical design choices to enable safe component optimization.
3.3.2. Surface Roughness
Surface roughness, residual stress, and microstructures also influence the mechanical properties. The relative
importance of these factors in determining the mechanical properties varies from material to material. Fatigue
properties of L-PBF materials are of particular importance in high-end applications such as aerospace and medical
devices. Researchers have demonstrated that surface roughness, inclusions and microstructure have a significant
effect on fatigue life [449]. As-built surfaces with high surface roughness exhibit poor fatigue properties regardless
of the defect populations, indicating that surface roughness is especially important in the context of fatigue.
Although machined samples with smooth surfaces did show poor fatigue properties, this was so only for those
that had many fusion pores [425,450]. Combining machined and low-porosity samples yields the best results.
Surface enhancement processes are highly important due to the subsurface pores and surface roughness of as-built
specimens. A variety of methods, including shot peening and laser shock peening, have been shown to enhance
mechanical properties [451]. It has been widely assumed that this improvement is due to the compressive stresses
at the surface and the improved surface finish, but recent research has demonstrated that pores near the surface
can also be closed through laser shock peening [452].
Surface roughness is characterised to a limited extent by most AM service providers (powder manufacturers and
machine manufacturers), but for qualiy assurance purposes in relation to this study, surface roughness
characteristics specific to the South African AM supply chain are quantified here. Evaluations were performed on
both cube samples (Sections 3.6.1 and 3.7.1) and overhang samples (Sections 3.6.2 and 3.7.2).
3.4. Unsupported Surfaces
Since the L-PBF process is layer-by-layer, the accuracy, surface detail and smoothness of parts produced by it are
limited. Heating and cooling at high rates can cause the materials to become stressed and distorted. Support
structures can be used as anchors and heat dissipators to reduce the risk of warpage in overhanging structures. In
an AM build platform, the design of the support structures affects the amount of deformation, build time, and
support removability of the parts. Support structures, when excessively utilized, can directly impact powder
consumption, as well as build and post-processing times. Additionally, surface irregularities resulting from
support removal can lead to more post-processing being required. As a result, it is highly valuable to find ways of
building L-PBF specimens without supports. Observations, however, have revealed that supported specimens
show significantly higher fatigue resistance than unsupported specimens [453]. When complex parts without
supports are manufactured, surfaces may be excessively rough on the downward side. When deformation occurs
during processing, it results in porosity and increased roughness of the surfaces, or even failure.
In AM processes, down-facing surfaces are often referred to as “downskins” and up-facing surfaces are referred
to as “upskins” both with reference to the build plate. Downskin surfaces are typically rougher than upskin or
vertical surfaces depending on the overhang angle. Because of this, and because it is not always possible to
machine or smooth complex or hidden surfaces, it is important to evaluate and optimize the surface finish for as


52
built components for different surface overhang angles. Generally, in the design of AM components with
overhangs, surface angles below the critical angle toward the build plate neccessitate the use of support structures.
The critical angle provided by most AM service providers is 45 degrees. While it is generally advised to use
support structures for surfaces under the provided critical angle, components can be built at lower overhang angles.
However, parts produced with angles under 45 degrees often have a poor surface quality due to heat accumulation
that causes powder to agglomerate on the underside.
It is certainly of importance and demand to develop AM processes that can produce complex geometric shapes
with overhang surface angles below 45 degrees without supporting structures, thus having better surface qualities.
In order to achieve the highest quality L-PBF parts with overhanging surfaces at low inclination angles, various
processes are being developed by AM machine manufacturers and AM service providers with the intent of
mitigating warpage, high surface roughness and defects on low-angle overhanging surfaces. This can be done
using special software, closed-loop control, and a special noncontact powder recoating systems [454]. This latter
method provides significant advantages over traditional L-PBF processes, but to fully explore its advantages, more
studies are needed.
In the context of this study, determining the characteristics of low-angle, unsupported surfaces on as-built AM
components would provide clarity on the surface characteristics of potential SAFFIRE engine components. This
is particularly relevant in the case of components having internal surfaces which are difficult or impossible to
machine (for example, internal fluid channels).
3.5. NDT Through X-Ray Tomography
In the recent past, X-ray microcomputed tomography (MicroCT) has become an established method for testing
and analysing AM-built components. Researchers and engineers are becoming increasingly interested in this non
destructive analysis method, but its capabilities are still not fully utilized and are still being developed. The use of
MicroCT is crucial for quantifying porosity values, pore distributions, and measuring dimensions non
destructively. Ideally, this process can be carried out before mechanical tests in order to pinpoint the impact of
defects - relating unexpected failures to certain types, locations, or distributions of defects. In addition to surface
roughness, residual stress and microstructure, other factors can influence as-built mechanical properties. The
relative importance of each of these factors varies with the material and cannot always be predicted [447,455].
The principle of MicroCT is to irradiate a sample with X-rays, measure the subsequent absorption X-ray image,
and acquire such images repeatedly as the sample rotates. Due to the penetration of X-rays, X-ray absorption
images provide a view of the sample from many angles. The acquired images can then be used in a mathematical
reconstruction to create a volumetric data set [456]. The volume is made up of voxels (volumetric pixels), with
the brightness of each pixel related to the density of the material it represents (X-ray density is determined by the
material’s density and effective atomic mass). Figure 3-1 illustrates the X-ray tomography process for the case of
an additively manufactured sample with an internal lattice. Powder is observed in the sample between the lattice
struts based on X-ray imaging results.


53
Figure 3-1: Illustration of the process of X-ray micro computed tomography taken from [457].
Once the data is scanned and reconstructed, it is visualized and analysed, typically using specialized software. A
more in-depth review of MicroCT technologies and the applications of such can be found in [455–457].
3.6. Methodology
A specific methodology was used to characterise the four AM materials evaluated in this study (Ti-6Al-4V, 316L,
MS1. 6061-T6), their respective manufacturing processes and the associated supply chains. This methodology
comprised the manufacture of standardized cube samples (for porosity and surface roughness analyses), overhang
samples (for surface roughness and defect analyses) and wall-thickness specimens to be individually analysed
using MicroCT methods. These analyses were performed with the intent of characterising defects or lack thereof
in AM materials for potential use in SAFFIRE engine components.
The MicroCT facility used was the Stellenbosch University CT Scanner Facility. As part of the Central Analytical
Facilities (CAF) of the university, it offers non-destructive X-ray computed tomography (CT) and high
performance image analysis services. A MicroCT scanner system (Figure 3-2) for large samples and a NanoCT
scanner for small samples are both available in the facility, both manufactured by General Electric Sensing and
Inspection Technologies/Phoenix X-ray. In this study, all samples smaller than 10 mm in the longest-axis
dimension were scanned using the NanoCT instrument. Larger samples, having longest-axis dimensions of 10
mm to 300 mm, were scanned using the MicroCT instrument. Further details on the facility and its capabilities
can be found at [458].


54
Figure 3-2: This photo illustrates the fundamental components of the setup used during the study. Taken from [459].
In a MicroCT scanner as shown in Figure 3-2, the x-rays are generated by an x-ray tube (A) that emitted x-rays
and transmitted through a sample (B), which is recorded by a detector (C). The resolution at which X-ray
MicroCTs can inspect AM-built parts non-destructively is limited by the size of the part. As a general rule,
MicroCT resolution scales linearly with part size. For example, a 30 mm part yields a 30 μm resolution, and a 5
mm part yields a 5 μm resolution. Because some types of defects in additive manufacturing may be small and
therefore missed in a scan of a large complex part, it is ideal to optimize processes before building critical parts.
Small test samples can be used in small-scale optimization by means of MicroCT.
For this study, various cube and overhang samples were manufactured using L-PBF on EOS M280 platforms
employed by a variety of service providers. Since EOS provides highly developed processing parameters for their
material catalogue, parts employing EOS-specific sample materials (i.e. Ti-6Al-4V, 316L, MS1) were
manufactured using the company’s standard processing parameters. While Elementum3D’s 6061-T6 powder is
designed to be manufactured on EOS platforms, it’s specific processing parameters for the EOS M280 are still
under refinement. The 6061-T6 samples were therefore manufactured using the processing parameters provided
by Elementum3D.
