Several scanning and analysis workflows and algorithms are possible for MicroCT-based porosity measurement.
This is due to the widespread use of MicroCT. The results of image processing can vary from one scan to another
based on the quality of the scan (whether there are artefacts, noise, image blurring, etc.), the type of de-noising
applied, and the analysis workflow engaged. The coupon sample used here is small enough to allow high
resolution and quality scanning, and in addition to assessing porosity and surface roughness, average density can
also be determined for the same sample. This is demonstrated in [460].
To accurately determine porosity through MicroCT techniques, specific image processing techniques need to be
used. The image processing steps are described here briefly; for more detail of the procedure, the reader is referred
to [461]. With these image processing steps, the aim is to remove the effects of exterior air, but include all
materials and air to separate the results of the surface roughness from the pores within the data set. Different
methods and software may be used to achieve this, since the goal is to create an accurate segmentation of the edge
of the cube, regardless of the size of internal pores or possible external noise particles. Segmentation is
accomplished by via the following proces:
1. Apply a basic automatic surface determination
2. Create an expanded (dilated) region of interest to eliminate surface roughness from the selection
3. Create a region of the air and noise particles surrounding the expanded region
4. Invert the region of air to select the entire part containing only internal pores
5. Advanced surface determination using the inverted region to achieve an accurate closed surface
containing only internal pores
6. Create a contracted (eroded) region of interest to again remove the surface roughness from the selection
The analysis of quantitative defects (porosity/voids) can be done using a number of methods, but the one
presented here works without a special algorithm, so it is less likely to yield errors from noisy data that is often
present in MicroCT scans. Using the region of interest determined from the above process, a porosity/void
analysis was performed using the “from defect ROI” setting. This analysis provides a vast amount of data with
respect to pore sizes, distributions and other criteria. The primary aim of these porosity analyses is to determine
the total percentage porosity and maximum pore size in the samples. Another important insight offered by
porosity analyses is the indication of contour porosity. Contour porosity is an accumulation of pores close to the
surface of a component and is a common defect in AM, often caused by incorrect processing parameters. Figure


57
3-4 shows a typical porosity analysis result with pores visualised. In this analysis, a significant amount of
contour porosity is indicated.
Figure 3-4: Porosity analysis indicating pore size. Contour porosity is clearly visible here.
In a number of recent studies of complex parts [462–464], surface topography was measured with MicroCT. As
built surface roughness analysis can be performed using MicroCT on cube coupon samples. Surface roughness is
historically measured with tactile probes or with non-contact interferometric techniques. However, AM surfaces
may be difficult to access and may be very rough, making it hard to accurately measure surface roughness. With
traditional techniques which are designed to measure smoother surfaces, it can be difficult to accurately measure
undercuts and deep, narrow troughs. In this study, the procedure used for determining surface roughness is
standardized and is only described briefly. Reference [465] contains the complete workflow details and results
accuracy information.
To obtain an accurate surface determination for surface roughness calculations, a similar process to the porosity
segmentation processes, mentioned previously, is used:
1. Apply a basic automatic surface determination
2. Create an expanded (dilated) region of interest to eliminate surface roughness from the selection
3. Create a region of the air and noise particles surrounding the expanded region
4. Invert the region of air to select the entire part containing only the external surface
5. Conduct advanced surface determination using the inverted region to achieve an accurate closed surface
and close all internal pores
For surface roughness measurements, specific areas must be analysed with respect to the mean flat surface in the
vicinity. For these analyses, a standard size of 3x3 mm for the analysis area is used on both the vertical and top
(opposite the AM build plate) surfaces. These surface areas are separated from the entire surface using regions of
interest placed roughly in the centre of each face. A flat-plane geometry element is then created to represent the
mean flat surface in the vicinity. This is done by using a best-fit surface measurement probe on a minimum of
fifteen points surrounding the region of interest. This flat surface can then be referenced in a “nominal/actual
comparison” analysis. This nominal/actual comparison calculates the deviation of the determined surface when
compared to the flat-plane geometry element in the region of interest specified. The analysis creates both a visual
result (Figure 3-5) and a significant amount of data on the deviation of each voxel from the mean surface.


