3.8.2. Powder Quality Control
Traditional powder analysis is performed using a laser diffraction method described in ASTM B822-17 [468].
While laser diffraction provides particle sizes, it assumes that particles are spherical regardless of the fact that
particles may often be not as spherical as expected. Using microscopy and image analysis, studies have examined
the morphology of metal powder particles, but the method can only obtain pseudo-3D images, which limits
qualitative analysis and does not reveal internal porosity.
Using synchrotron tomography, researchers have demonstrated that porosity within powder particles can be
transferred to the melt pool and consequently to the final product [469]. Additionally, particle size distribution
and sphericity influence powder flowability, in turn influencing the characteristic of the powder bed, such as
spreading and packing density.
Performing MicroCT scans on AM powders is an effective way to characterise various specific properties of AM
materials. The methods and purposes of scanning powders via MicroCT are described in detail in [470,471].
MicroCT methods typically require little to no sample preparation, reducing the complexity and therefore cost of
scanning powders. For the purposes of this study, the methods used in [470] were applied to two sets of 6061-T6
powders.


74
In AM processes, the un-melted powder present in the build chamber is often recycled to various degrees, and
different quality control methods are applied dependant on powder and AM machine characteristics. Metal powder
analysis has been used to detect changes in powder quality after repeated re-use. It was shown in [472], for
instance, that powder particles become less spherical as the number of re-use cycles increases, and that their
surface becomes increasingly rougher. Additionally, other types of powder may partially fuse, which decreases
flowability in the powder bed process. Re-used powder should be characterized in any case to ensure that its
properties are maintained.
After the findings of porosity defects in Section 3.7, the AM service provider employed for manufacture of the
6061-T6 samples conveyed that the powder batch from which the samples were made had undergone a large
number of re-use cycles. This finding led to a quick investigation into the powder characteristics associated with
all samples manufactured, including those discussed in Section 3.8.1. In support of this investigation, a new batch
of 6061-T6 was ordered from the powder supplier and samples of both this new powder and the old powder were
analysed using MicroCT techniques. This effectively enabled a comparison of the 6061-T6 powder in two states:
new powder with no re-use cycles, and old powder with high re-use cycles. Both powders were scanned using the
method described in [470].
After MicroCT scanning, the powders were analysed with respect to each other. However, due to many inclusions
(high-density particles) present in both powders’ samples, the MicroCT scan quality was poor, as can be seen in
Figure 3-18, and very little quantitative data could be extracted on this basis. In MicroCT, these higher-density
inclusions distort the X-rays and cause scattering in the vicinity. Since powder scans using these techniques require
a particularly good scan quality to determine powder sphericity, no quantitative data of this nature could be
obtained. It was however found that the inclusions present in the sample of old powder (Figure 3-18, left) were
far more numerous, and thus the scan quality was significantly poorer. This suggests that a higher quantity of
high-density particles was present in the old powder sample, which could be a possible cause of the defects
described throughout Sections 3.7 and 3.8.1.


75
Figure 3-18: 6061-T6 powder MicroCT scan images of both old (left) and new (right) powder samples.
Due to the proprietary chemical composition of the 6061-T6 powder, it is difficult to arrive at definitive
conclusions concerning the nature of the differences between powder samples. Of significance, however, the
powder supplier did indicate that an unknown ceramic forms ≈2% of the powder’s composition (see Appendix G:
Elementum3D 6061-T6 RAM2 Powder/Material Specifications). It is well known that Aluminium 6061, in its
traditional chemical composition, manufactures very poorly on AM systems. The incorporation of ceramics in
difficult-to-manufacture AM materials is an emerging technique used to enhance associated manufacturing
characteristics. Since the density and sphericity of these ceramics often differ from those of the a powder’s metallic
alloying elements, it is possible that through repeated re-use, the ratio of ceramic content to metallic content within
such powders may change. Some assumptions can be made with respect to this information:
1. Ceramic particles may be larger or smaller than metallic particles, and may therefore get separated from the
powder during the sieving phase of the re-use process
2. During mixing phase, the higher density metallic particles and the lower density ceramic particles may not
necessarily become evenly distributed
There are also some other potential causes for these high-density inclusions in the powder samples. One of these
could be the partial fusion of metallic particles that occurs during repeated use. Over time, this could result in an
accumulation of particle clusters. Partially fused or fully fused particles have a higher effective density than virgin
powder, and as such, they would be detected as inclusions.
The results of this investigation highlight key potential causes of defects in AM processes which stem from
inadequate powder quality control. Specific quality control measures should be established to ensure that powder
quality does not reduce over repeated re-use cycles. This could, for instance, take the form of periodic MicroCT
analyses of both powder and cube samples to detect defects which may arise from repeated powder re-use.


