3.9. Conclusions and Recommendations
Standardized testing procedures were applied to quantitatively analyse AM-built samples for the Ti-6Al-4V, MS1,
316L and 6061-T6 materials candidates considered, allowing for direct comparison of material properties and
characterisation of defects. Detailed analyses were performed to gather the porosity, surface roughness and
unsupported surface characteristics for each material. 10 mm and 5 mm cubes provided insight into the nature of
porosity defects in each material and further process optimization was performed (Section 3.8.1) to clarify the
possible mitigation and quality control procedures which can be applied when poor porosity characteristics are
identified. These porosity distributions extend into larger more complex parts (overhang samples) as well. Surface
roughness analyses showed both the comparison of surface characteristics between materials, and the correlation
of surface roughness variations with varying unsupported surface inclination angles. The geometry deviation
characteristics of materials was quantified for overhang samples, which allowed for insights into cross-material
deviation. AM powder quality control methods were outlined with suggestions for future quality control
methodologies. A wall thickness sample was analysed to gain an understanding of the design limits for AM
materials related to the AM supply chain in question.
Recommendations for further work:
The effects of heat-treatment processes on porosity and deviation are not analysed here and could be studied
further for each material. Post-processing such as Hot-Isostatic Pressing (HIP) are known to improve porosity
characteristics and could be applied to contour, bulk and subsurface porosity present on all materials if the design
requires. Further improvement of porosity characteristics may be possible for all materials through the iterative
process parameter optimization outlined in Section 3.8.1. Some AM materials were found to feature inclusions,
and further analyses of these inclusions may lead to correlations for porosity measurements.
Overhang samples showed defects such as drooping effects or bulbous features and further testing with optimized
samples may enable reduced surface roughness and geometric deviation. Furthermore, overhang samples could
be modified by varying wall thicknesses at each inclination angle, to give insight into the effects of wall thickness
variations on both surface roughness and near-surface porosity for different inclination angles. Although literature
suggests that near-surface porosity is not correlated to inclination angle, further quantitative analyses could be
performed to compare the near-surface porosity characteristics of different AM materials. Deviation analyses on
unsupported surfaces could be performed on a per-inclination angle basis to provide data and correlations relating
to the specific warpage characteristics of AM materials. Due to the relatively high-quality material characteristics
found at low inclination angles, further testing could be undertaken at lower inclination angles. MicroCT surface
roughness results may also be further verified through traditional measurement techniques, such as the use of
tactile probes or non-contact interferometric techniques.
In optimising the AM process parameters for 6061-T6 aluminium, higher-quality porosity characteristics were
demonstrated than those achieved using process parameters provided by the AM service provider. The
corresponding changes may affect mechanical properties and should therefore be tested.


