4. PROCESS CHARACTERIZATION OF ADDITIVELY MANUFACTURED PUMP
COMPONENTS
4.1. Introduction
Aerospace industry segments, including commercial and military aircraft, space applications, and missile systems
are transforming with the use of additive manufacturing (AM) technologies. The AM process has enabled such a
transformation because it is able to fabricate parts with complex designs and reduce production costs (material
waste, assembly costs due to part consolidation, and the need for tools and fixtures). In addition, AM processes
are able to produce parts in small quantities with short turnaround times and low cost. Through AM, advanced
part designs can be realized, such as multifunctional parts, multi-material parts, consolidated parts, and difficult
to-machine parts. AM is ideal for the space industry due to its ability to fabricate freeform designs in short lead
times.
The AM of metal components is promising in industrial applications due to the high complexity of part geometries
that can be achieved, allowing improved performance to be realized for functional components, including
lightweighting, part consolidation, the minimization of joints and connections between different parts, and
allowing shorter lead times and easing design iterations. Despite the high cost of once-off part manufacturing, the
above advantages find niche applications in specific industries such as the space industry, as discussed in [473]
and Section 2.3.
The growth of the space industry in recent years is especially driven by the increased demand for small and
medium sized satellites. This demand has led to the increased use of metal AM to manufacture various rocket
components including combustion chambers with complex geometries. Due to the vast complexity of rocket
engine components and the high performance required for optimal function, such components are traditionally
tested extensively via many iterations. Using traditional manufacturing techniques, this has historically been a
remarkably expensive endeavour. With the advantages of design complexity come new challenges in quality
control and inspection. Metal AM processes are highly complex, with numerous interrelated parameters and
significant potential for manufacturing errors or flaws, including internal defects (various forms of porosity,
inclusions, cracks, residual stresses, unwanted microstructures) as well as high surface roughness and dimensional
errors. Typically, process optimization is combined with quality control efforts to ensure high quality parts are
produced as discussed in Section 2.2.5. High quality here refers to mechanical properties reaching at least the
minimum levels as set out by aerospace standards. The presence of manufacturing flaws will compromise
mechanical properties and may have catastrophic consequences, if undetected.
Traditional non-destructive evaluation (NDE) methods are not well suited to metal AM, as the typical flaw sizes
are much smaller than in traditionally manufactured components. Furthermore, the typical complexity of the parts,
the rough surfaces and the unique microstructures all play roles in limiting the efficiency of traditional NDE
methods [474]. X-ray micro computed tomography (MicroCT) is well established and has proven to be best suited
to the inspection of such parts, as concluded in [457]. While the method is used widely, it is mainly used for small
objects of low-density metals such as aluminium and titanium alloys. This is due to one of the well-known
challenges faced by this method: it requires sufficient X-ray penetration of the evaluation object, which limits its


82
use for dense materials such as steel. When an object is too dense, the resulting MicroCT images are noisy and
contain image artifacts, limiting further data processing and the ability to make a reliable evaluation. Due to these
reasons, it is widely considered impossible to evaluate large, high-density components using typical laboratory
MicroCT systems (225 kV, typically). High-power CT systems with voltages exceeding 300 kV as well as
synchrotron tomography beamlines (with high flux) are better-suited but are not widely available.
Rapid prototyping, rapid tooling, and repairs are some of the most important applications of AM in the space
industry, together with final part manufacturing of metal, plastic, ceramic, and composite parts. Final part
manufacturing is the application with the fastest growth currently. AM-fabricated components can be designed
and manufactured on demand with significant mass and cost-reductions, without sacrificing their mechanical
properties. In the space industry, rapid prototyping plays an important role. Despite the growing prevalence of
virtual prototyping, rapid prototyping remains essential for activities such as evaluating and testing design
concepts iteratively and at low cost compared to traditional techniques. A primary advantage of using AM for
rapid prototyping is the reduction of time-to-market by accelerating prototyping, the reduction of development
costs, and the possibility of increasing innovation through design complexity enabled by AM design.
For the purposes of the SAFFIRE programme, utilizing AM for both rapid-prototyping and final part
manufacturing can enable significant cost reductions and performance enhancements via mass reduction and part
consolidation. This is especially advantageous for components where specific performance and operational
characteristics are unknown, and iterative design and testing is required to both verify and optimize the
components. The flaws present in AM processes are well-studied and common practices are available for design
for additive manufacturing (DfAM) activities. However, these practices are limited and the specific geometry of
the components in question heavily affects the occurrence of defects, especially in cases where highly complex
geometry is present.
The primary focus of this chapter is to evaluate the AM manufacturing process for functional rocket engine
propellant pump components under development in the SAFFIRE programme. These pump components are
iteratively designed and evaluated through NDE techniques to assess the quality of the manufacturing process and
to inform designers of the possible design complexities achievable through AM, and the benefits of doing so.
4.2. Liquid Rocket Engine Propellent Pumps
At present, South Africa, and Africa as a whole, lacks the capacity to independently launch payloads into Low
Earth Orbit (LEO). In response to this capacity shortfall, the Aerospace Systems Research Group (ASReG) of
the University of KwaZulu-Natal is incrementally establishing the technology building blocks that are required to
develop an indigenous orbital launch vehicle. In this regard, the first major challenge to overcome is the
development of the propulsion systems required by such a vehicle. The SAFFIRE liquid rocket engine
development programme was initiated by ASReG as a means of doing so [475,476].
In the majority of traditional liquid rocket engines, the propellants are pressurized by turbopump systems prior to
being admitted to the combustion chamber. Standard engine system cycles include the gas generator, staged
combustion, and expander cycles. Turbines are used in all these systems to drive the pumps mechanically through
a shaft. What differentiates these systems is how these turbines are powered.


83
With the recent advancements in battery technology, the SAFFIRE programme intends to use an alternative
approach. Rather than relying on a turbine, high-speed electric motors will be used to drive the propellant pumps.
Figure 4-1 shows the SAFFIRE engine configuration with electric motor driven propellent pumps (left) and the
full stack of 9 engines (right) on a concept launch vehicle [475]. The electrically-driven pumps have been referred
to as ‘electropumps’ which, under certain circumstances, have many advantages over turbopumps. In this case,
there is no need for the turbine, gas generator/pre-combustor, drive shaft, and any gearboxes which all add
significant mass to the rocket engine. However, these electropumps require significantly powerful battery systems
which are heavy, especially when larger scale engines are considered. Simplified system control, start-up
sequences and engine throttling are key advantages of electropump-based engines. The Rutherford engine is the
most prominent example of a electropump-driven liquid rocket engine that is actively used to launch payloads
into orbit. This engine is used to propel Rocket Lab's Electron launch vehicle [4].
AM, while still a relatively new manufacturing technology, is being used extensively in spacecraft applications
and almost all in-development liquid rocket engine systems use this technology to some degree. The most
prominent examples of this are the SpaceX Raptor, the Rocket Lab Rutherford and the Launcher E-2 rocket
engines. Each of these engines feature substantial use of AM; for example, SpaceX’s Raptor engine contains as
much as 40% by-mass AM fabricated components (Section 2.3.4). Rocket Lab’s Rutherford engine (Figure 4-2)
in particular uses AM for the majority of its components too, including the combustion chamber, nozzle, valves,
pipes and propellent pumps.
Propellent pumps, which typically consist of a centrifugal-type impeller and a corresponding volute, are high
complexity components which typically require advanced manufacturing methods to achieve the geometrical
quality needed for performance. The first-iteration SAFFIRE engine, currently under development, features two
propellant pumps; one liquid oxygen (LOX) pump and one kerosene pump. Both pump systems have different
performance characteristics in terms of hydrodynamic power, rotational speed of the impeller and mass flow rates,
and therefore, each pump system requires an individual design. Both the LOX and kerosene pump systems
comprise a pair of impellers and volutes which have vastly different designs due to the different working fluids.
Initial hydrodynamic analyses and evaluation of conceptual LOX and kerosene pump systems were previously
undertaken as discussed in [477,478]. Following the initial conceptualizations, small-scale electropump systems
were designed, manufactured and tested in two projects concurrently as described in this chapter, and in [479].
The focus of this chapter is to describe the AM processes used, design for additive manufacturing (DfAM)
techniques and MicroCT-based inspection techniques primarily for quality control of final components. The focus
of the work described in [479] was hydrodynamic design, manufacturing and testing of these small-scale
electropump components to determine performance characteristics.


