Volute and impeller components, which are the primary components of a pump system, are traditionally
manufactured using methods such as investment casting and 5-axis CNC machining. Centrifugal pump impellers


85
are relatively small and highly geometrically complex components. The design of pump impellers poses a variety
of manufacturing challenges in the pumps industry [482]. Additionally, there is a size limit below which impellers
are no longer manufacturable with any conventional method, mostly since investment casting is not suitable for
3D geometries at very small scales.
Volutes are the fixed elements of centrifugal pumps and are used to direct the fluid output, while concurrently
decreasing the speed and increasing the static pressure of the fluid. While volutes can be manufactured by 5-axis
machining methods, casting methods are generally used due to the relative costs associated with mass
manufacturing. However, these costs move in favour of 5-axis machining methods at lower production volumes
and under prototyping due to the large costs associated with casting tooling.
AM techniques provide benefits for manufacturing impeller and volute geometries in small-scale production runs,
and for rapid prototyping. This is primarily due to the low costs associated with tooling requirements when using
AM methods. The manufacturing lead times for AM enables fast iterations of testing components, and batch
manufacturing of functional parts. Furthermore, the geometric complexity permitted by AM techniques allows for
more complex, higher-performance designs to be realised.
The minimum size of pump components manufactured via traditional processes (casting and 5-axis CNC
machining) is constrained by the difficulties associated with producing complex shapes at small scales, including
the requirement of tooling. Conversely, AM can produce very complex geometries at small-scales and AM-built
small-scale components often have better quality compared to casting methods and non-machined surfaces. This
is limited, however, by the minimum achievable resolution of the AM process and material combination, which
is usually around 0.3 mm for L-PBF systems.
Material selection for pump components plays an important role in the performance that can achieved.
Components of LOX pumps in particular have many associated risks, such as cryogenic temperature failures and
LOX ignition events with some materials. LOX systems are subject to extremely low temperatures and proper
material selection is essential as the correct material for the application can reduce the probability of system
failure.
The mechanical properties of metals at cryogenic temperatures varies greatly. Certain materials weaken upon
cryogenic cooling, while others can strengthen significantly. Marked changes in toughness can also occur, with
appreciable embrittlement being more common than not. In addition, pump components exposed to LOX must be
manufactured from materials possessing a degree of oxygen compatibility sufficient for exposure to high-pressure
environments where part rubbing is a possibility.
Some materials are much better-suited than others in respect of the above requirements. From the findings of
[477,479], Inconel 718 was selected as the optimal LOX pump system material and is considered for the flight
SAFFIRE engine configuration. Kerosene pump components are not subject to the same level of material
compatibility constraints compared to those of LOX pumps, and a vast range of materials are available for use.
Ti-6Al-4V was selected as the optimal material choice for the kerosene pump system due to its excellent specific
strength characteristics.